The system has multiple types of users, rather than one generic dashboard: platform administrators, tutors/staff, school administrators, students and parents. Some student accounts are individual learners; others are associated with schools or parent-managed enrolments.

That complexity is central to understanding why the work has been more involved than a normal website redesign. A change to a student lookup, school identifier, examination permission or parent relationship can affect several portals simultaneously.

# 3. Chronological development history

## Phase 1 — August 2026: Firestore database migration and alignment

20 August

One of the critical infrastructure decisions documented in the history was the migration from Firestore's default database to a named Firestore database.

The database in question is:

`ai-studio-jaystarblissdyna-085e16ac-52ee-43ae-9c0c-52f6db7f8f7c`

The Firebase project is `jaystarbliss-studios`.

### What changed

The website's Firebase client configuration was using the named database through a call equivalent to:

`getFirestore(app, "ai-studio-jaystarblissdyna-085e16ac-52ee-43ae-9c0c-52f6db7f8c")`

The important distinction is that the browser and server must use the same Firestore database. Using the correct Firebase project alone is not enough if one side queries the default database and the other side queries a named one.

The migration work was intended to preserve the target database's data, recursively copy collections and subcollections where required, and leave the original default database untouched.

### Why it mattered

The application has both browser-side Firebase access and trusted server-side operations. If these point to different databases, the system can exhibit confusing failures:

- A student appears to exist in the database but cannot log in.
- The browser can read some information while a server function cannot find it.
- New records appear to have been created successfully but are unavailable to another workflow.
- Authentication appears to reject valid credentials when the underlying record lookup is the real failure.

The migration also made it necessary to check for accidental default-database initializations elsewhere in the codebase.

Critical lesson: database alignment is a system-wide requirement, not merely a configuration detail in one file.

## Phase 2 — 3–5 September: Backend repair, security hardening and real-data integrity

This was the first substantial series of documented changes in the repository.

### 2.1 Repairing Firebase Admin and Netlify Functions

On 3 September, [PR #2 — Repair Netlify Firebase Admin Functions](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/2?utm_source=chatgpt.com) addressed a production build failure.

The build was failing because a required server-side module, `api/_lib/firebase-admin.ts`, could not be resolved by Netlify's function bundler.

The repair included:

- Restoring the missing Firebase Admin initializer.
- Adding `firebase-admin` to production dependencies.
- Declaring Node.js 22 for the runtime.
- Configuring the Netlify Functions directory.

Why: the frontend compiling successfully did not mean the backend functions were ready to run. Student access, school access and other protected operations depend on server functions that must bundle and deploy correctly.

The security principle was also important: Firebase Admin credentials belong on the server, not in publicly served browser code.

### 2.2 Removing fabricated dashboard data

[PR #3 — Security, real-data integrity and production readiness](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/3?utm_source=chatgpt.com) followed on 3 September.

This addressed an important credibility problem: dashboard interfaces contained fabricated student progress, admin metrics, school telemetry and fallback chart values.

The changes removed these fabricated values and tightened several access boundaries:

- Student and parent notifications were scoped to the intended recipients.
- Firestore rules for notifications, invitations, exams and enrolment requests were tightened.
- `SUPER_ADMIN` authority was separated from delegated administrator permissions.
- Client-created user records could no longer simply grant themselves privileged admin roles.
- Delegated administrators were restricted from changing protected role and school-ownership fields.
- School passcode records were updated to retain school ownership.

### Why we made these changes

A dashboard showing plausible numbers is not necessarily a functioning dashboard. If a metric is hardcoded, the user cannot rely on it to represent actual students, progress or transactions.

The goal was to make the platform's displayed information correspond to real records and ensure users could not gain authority merely by changing client-side data.

This principle continued into later wallet, school-reporting and examination work.

### 2.3 Establishing a consistent design system

[PR #4 — UI/UX Pro Max design system](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/4?utm_source=chatgpt.com) was merged on 3 September.

It introduced a repository-level design-system foundation and an additional CSS refinement layer.

The work included:

- Brand, elevation and focus tokens.
- Clearer dashboard hierarchy and card treatments.
- Improved focus visibility and touch behaviour.
- More responsive forms and text wrapping.
- Reduced-motion support and mobile safe-area handling.

Why: the platform contains many dashboards and shared components. Without consistent visual rules, fixes in one area can create a different visual experience elsewhere. A reusable design system was intended to make later interface changes more predictable.

## Phase 3 — 4–10 September: Making academic workflows and permissions real

The next phase concentrated on connecting the parts of the learning platform.

### 3.1 Assignments, submissions, grading and feedback

The audit on 4–5 September identified missing links in the academic workflow: assignment lifecycle management, grading and feedback, milestones, communication, attendance and assessment-progress integration.

The implementation documented in commit `145a6ba898abcc3399cbc75f7e43f22c7a3988e7` added or connected several priority workflows:

- Tutors could create and publish assignments.
- Students could submit their work.
- Tutors could grade submissions and provide feedback.
- Resubmission could be requested.
- Parent and student notifications were connected to relevant actions.
- School monitoring, milestones and attendance-related capabilities were developed.

The intent was to make these workflows operate across the appropriate student, parent, staff and school portals instead of leaving them as disconnected screens.

### 3.2 Student access and school boundaries

A series of September changes then focused on ensuring each user saw the correct records.

Examples include:

- [PR #5 — Parent accounts, billing assignment and seeded-data removal](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/5?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
- [PR #9 — Student access scope and portal navigation](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/9?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
- [PR #10 — Student schedule scope and parent enrolment flow](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/10?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

The guiding rules were:

- Platform super administrators could operate across the platform.
- School administrators should be limited to learners belonging to their school.
- Tutors and staff should see students within their assigned school or direct private-tutoring relationships.
- Students should see their own schedules and permitted school/class information.
- Parents should access records connected to their children rather than being able to request arbitrary learner records.

These boundaries were applied to credential issuance, schedule retrieval and other student-facing functions as well as to the interface.

### 3.3 Parent account provisioning

PR #5 introduced a protected `admin-parent-family` Netlify Function to create or provision parent accounts through Firebase Admin and link children using real student records.

It also addressed a dangerous form of false success: parent programme, tuition, billing-cycle and tutor assignments previously used `Promise.allSettled()` in a way that could allow the interface to report success even if some writes failed.

The changes made failed writes visible rather than silently treating incomplete operations as successful.

Why this mattered: parent enrolment is not just a form submission. It creates relationships that other features rely on, including billing, schedules and parent access to academic information. Those relationships must be persisted consistently.

