## Phase 6 — 15–16 October: Integrating the examination system

This was one of the largest feature additions in the recorded history.

The goal was to integrate a comprehensive, MegaExams-style examination workflow inside the existing Jaystarbliss Studios/Jaystarbliss Dynamic Institute platform, not to build a disconnected examination website with a separate user database.

### 6.1 The examination workflow

[PR #24 — Comprehensive examination workflow and portal navigation](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/24?utm_source=chatgpt.com) was merged on 16 October.

It covered the student, tutor/staff and administrator sides of examinations.

### Student examination journey

1. See upcoming, active and completed examinations.
2. Validate the examination passcode and verify school, class or individual assignment eligibility.
3. Start a timed attempt governed by server-side timing and attempt limits.
4. Save answers, including automatic saves, and submit the attempt.
5. Receive automatic marking for supported objective questions and manual marking where required.
6. View results and tutor feedback only after authorised release.

### Tutor and platform administrator journey

1. Create examinations, prepare questions and maintain a question bank.
2. Assign exams to classes or individual learners and configure passcodes and schedules.
3. Preview and activate exams, then manage attempts and grading.
4. Correct answer keys, mark manually, release results and review examination history.
5. Produce reports and exports, including individual PDF reports and bulk exports.

### 6.2 Why server-side controls were essential

The examination work included several protections that are not merely cosmetic:

- Passcode validation and eligibility checks happen on the trusted server.
- Examination opening and closing times are enforced against server timestamps.
- Attempt limits and activation windows are checked.
- Answer keys remain hidden before result release.
- Race-safe transactions prevent a timed-out or submitted attempt from being overwritten by a late save.
- Published examination settings cannot simply be reset to draft to reopen a completed exam.
- Examination data and passcode hashes are protected by server-side role checks and Firestore rules.

The system also includes manual grading, explicit result release, audit records and notifications.

Why: a timed exam has to remain consistent even if the browser is slow, a network request arrives late or a student submits while an autosave is still in progress. The server must be the authority on whether an attempt is open, submitted, expired or released.

### 6.3 School reporting and progress monitoring

[PR #25 — School role scope and learner progress reporting](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/25?utm_source=chatgpt.com) followed on 16 October.

The School portal was adjusted toward a reporting workspace with:

- Class and student filters.
- Assessment and examination history.
- Attendance and activity timelines.
- Individual learner PDF reports.
- School-specific access checks.

A particularly important change was distinguishing the school's canonical `schoolId` from a human-readable school code. A display code is useful to people, but the application needs a consistent identifier to determine record ownership.

The server must reject a request for a learner who is not part of the authenticated school's roster.

### 6.4 Parent examination results

[PR #26 — Examination parity across the portals](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/26?utm_source=chatgpt.com) extended the examination system to parents.

It added a read-only parent results view for linked children, with the parent identity derived from a verified Firebase ID token. The endpoint returns only results that have been explicitly released.

The design intentionally does not trust a learner ID supplied by the browser to determine which child's results a parent may access.

It also improved tutor/admin question authoring through pasted-question templates and validation for question numbering, answer keys, options and objective-answer mappings.

### 6.5 Grading and result privacy

[PR #27 — Assessment grading, privacy and role boundaries](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/27?utm_source=chatgpt.com) tightened the integrity of examination data.

The work included:

- Validating question definitions before persistence.
- Rejecting duplicate question IDs, invalid options, unsupported question types and marks outside permitted ranges.
- Using the same grading logic for normal submission and scheduled expiry.
- Restricting answer keys, explanations and raw student responses from school-level reports.
- Binding parent result lookup to both the linked learner record and the learner's portal UID.
- Failing closed when a learner record ID is ambiguous across legacy collections.

This was necessary because incorrect question data can lead to incorrect grades, and a school report should not accidentally expose the examination's answer key.

### 6.6 First-test readiness and mobile usability

[PR #28 — Assessment first-test readiness](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/28?utm_source=chatgpt.com) fixed additional role and timing issues. It included support for the portal's individual-student role aliases, deadline handling and serialised answer autosaves.

[PR #29 — Assessment submission and mobile portal UX](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/29?utm_source=chatgpt.com) addressed a particularly important timeout edge case: unsaved browser answers submitted after the deadline must not replace answers already persisted by the server.

It also improved narrow-screen layouts with fluid content, single-column grids, wrapped action groups, compact spacing, scrollable tab bars and mobile navigation for parent examination results.

### What was verified at this stage

The PR #29 record reports that the Assessment Quality Gate passed 11 assessment tests, lint, serverless-function typechecking and the production build. The existing Quality Gate and Firestore Rules Tests also passed.

However, the recorded deployment notes still flagged Netlify preview failure and stated that real-account end-to-end acceptance was not yet verified. Those are separate requirements from passing automated tests.

## Phase 7 — 19–22 September: Wallets, billing, withdrawals and payment integrity

The financial work was driven by a clear requirement: do not show fictional money, transactions, account identities or payout states as if they were real.

The major dashboard and withdrawal overhaul is documented in commit `a830cdcf6e88afec310fd9b214906242a57300fd`.

### What changed

| Area                    | Change and reason                                                                                                                 |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Wallets                 | Move balances and transaction history toward Firebase-backed records rather than hardcoded demo figures.                          |
| Fake data               | Remove fabricated names, demo transactions and misleading wallet or verification cards.                                           |
| Withdrawals             | Restrict the withdrawal flow to bank accounts instead of retaining OPay/MoMo options that were not part of the intended workflow. |
| Nigerian bank directory | Add searchable, paginated bank-directory retrieval through Paystack.                                                              |
| Account resolution      | Add a bank-account resolution workflow to verify the selected account details.                                                    |
| Saved accounts          | Support saving bank-account details for later use.                                                                                |
| Impersonation           | Correct identity display and dark-mode contrast while an administrator is impersonating another account.                          |
| Terminology             | Replace “Cadets” with “Students” in the relevant user-facing interfaces.                                                          |
| Billing labels          | Remove stale or misleading billing labels and payout UI.                                                                          |

The work was intended to make the financial interface reflect actual application records and the intended payment-provider workflow.

A wallet balance, however, is only genuinely trustworthy when the underlying transaction lifecycle is also correct. A UI connected to Firestore does not, by itself, prove that deposits, earnings, deductions and withdrawals are reconciled properly. Those need their own end-to-end verification.

### Netlify compilation problems that followed

The initial September build fixes were followed by further TypeScript failures. On 21–22 September, missing imports and type-only import problems were corrected. After that, older `'bank' | 'opay'` type definitions in `AdminBilling.tsx` and `BillingCenter.tsx` still conflicted with the new bank-only withdrawal flow.

Additional fixes were committed to align those interfaces with the intended payout model.

The lesson was that a change to a shared financial type can break several screens even when the main withdrawal component has already been updated. Every consumer of that type must be checked before calling the workflow complete.

## Phase 8 — 19 October: Resolving the Netlify secrets-scanning failure

[PR #30 — Netlify deploy error due to exposed Firebase secrets in build output](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/30?utm_source=chatgpt.com) was merged on 19 October.

The build had been failing because Netlify's secrets scanner was flagging the Firebase project ID in tracked files or the build output.

The investigation distinguished two very different kinds of values:

- Firebase web configuration: the project ID and Firestore database ID are used by the browser application and are not equivalent to private service-account credentials.
- Private server credentials: the Firebase service-account private key and client email must remain protected and must not be exposed in public client bundles.

The fix configured the scanner to skip only the public project and database identifiers, with an explanation for the exception. The real private credentials remained subject to scanning.

### Why this was important

The wrong solution would have been to remove the public Firebase configuration indiscriminately and break the site's Firebase connection.

The other wrong solution would have been to disable secrets scanning entirely.

The targeted exception was intended to preserve the security check for actual secrets while avoiding a false positive on values that the web application necessarily uses.

The recorded investigation also noted domain-restriction hardening for the public Firebase web API key as an optional security improvement.

## Phase 9 — 22 October: The student login incident and successive repairs

This is the most recent and most operationally critical part of the history.

The student portal login problem is not a single isolated code defect in the available records. It developed across several interacting changes involving server-side authentication, legacy student records, database selection and error handling.

### 9.1 The earlier security change

On 3 September, student access-code verification was moved to the trusted server. A subsequent change on 5 September introduced SHA-256 verification for normalised access codes.

The security objective was valid: student credentials and privileged Firestore operations should not depend on unrestricted browser-side reads or client-side account creation.

The complication was backward compatibility. Over time, student records had been created using different field names, username formats, access-code storage formats and profile-linking patterns.

The historical login implementation had supported several of those older formats.

### 9.2 PR #33 — Correcting the database and credential lookup

[PR #33 — Fix onboarded student login and named Firestore database access](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/33?utm_source=chatgpt.com) was merged on 22 October.

It addressed several problems:

- Firebase Admin could query the default Firestore database while the browser was using the named database.
- New student records could contain only an `accessCodeHash`.
- Username casing and legacy field names were not always handled.
- Some older records linked student details through `users/{uid}`.
- The login interface had a browser-side account-creation and anonymous-auth fallback that could produce `auth/admin-restricted-operation`.

The repair aligned the server-side database configuration, expanded the lookup logic and removed the insecure browser-side fallback.

Why the fallback was removed: student login should not attempt to create or elevate Firebase accounts in the browser simply because the trusted authentication endpoint failed. That hides the actual problem and can introduce a separate authentication failure.

### 9.3 PR #34 — Restoring legacy credential compatibility

[PR #34 — Restore legacy-compatible student portal login](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/34?utm_source=chatgpt.com) was merged later on 22 October.

The next investigation found that correcting the database selection was not sufficient to guarantee compatibility with existing records.

The repair expanded the trusted server-side lookup to handle historical variations, including:

- Canonical and legacy username/email fields.
- Linked student and user profiles.
- Older access-code, passcode, code and password fields.
- SHA-256 hashes generated from exact or uppercase-normalised input.
- Legacy records with historical field arrangements.
- Multiple candidate records, so an early username match would not automatically prevent checking the correct credential match.

It also retained account-status and role checks.

The important design decision was to restore the old compatibility behaviour inside the trusted server, rather than reopening unrestricted student-record reads in the browser.

### 9.4 PR #35 — Restoring the bounded legacy scan

[PR #35 — Restore legacy student login lookup](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/35?utm_source=chatgpt.com) was merged on 22 October.

Some older student records could still be missed by exact-field queries because their usernames or email fields used unusual casing or formatting.

The repair added a bounded fallback scan across the relevant student collections. The lookup uses the normal exact-field and compatibility paths first, then falls back to the scan when those paths fail.

The security requirement remained unchanged: credential verification and token issuance stay on the trusted server. The repair did not restore public Firestore reads or client-side account creation.

### 9.5 PR #36 — Correcting the database mismatch and false invalid-credential responses

[PR #36 — Fix student login Firestore database mismatch](https://github.com/jaystarbliss-dynamic-hub/V3-JAYSTARBLISS-STUDIOS-MAIN-WEBSITE/pull/36?utm_source=chatgpt.com) was merged on 22 October.

This addressed another critical distinction: a failed database lookup is not necessarily a wrong password or access code.

The previous query helper could suppress Firestore lookup failures and return a response that looked like invalid credentials even when the underlying database query had failed.

The final repair in this sequence:

- Pinned Firebase Admin to the exact named Firestore database used by the browser application.
- Added sanitised warnings when environment variables disagree with that configuration.
- Recorded Firestore query failures without logging student credentials or access-code hashes.
- Returned a server error when every primary lookup query failed, rather than falsely reporting invalid credentials.
- Kept sensitive lookup and authentication operations server-side.

The merge commit is `cbb7b6ddaa477464e78fc12835220ff6560b851c`.

