### What the login sequence tells us

Correct direction: security

Moving credential verification to a trusted server and keeping Firestore rules restrictive was the appropriate security direction.

Compatibility problem: historical data

Existing and newly onboarded students did not necessarily have identical record structures. Exact-field lookup alone was insufficient for every historical record.

Infrastructure problem: database selection

Browser and server-side Firebase code could query different Firestore databases, causing valid records to appear missing.

Diagnostic problem: misleading errors

Suppressing lookup failures could make a database or configuration problem look like a student entering incorrect credentials.

The lesson is that all four layers must work together: database selection, record discovery, credential verification and session issuance. Fixing only one layer does not prove the whole login journey is healthy.

Most importantly, I will not claim that the latest merge proves every existing and newly registered student can log in. The repository records confirm the changes and automated checks, but the latest history does not establish a successful production sign-in for both an existing and a newly onboarded student.

# 4. Critical technical decisions across the project

The following decisions recur throughout the development history because they affect multiple portals.

| Decision                                               | Why it was made                                                          | What it protects or improves                                                     |
| ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Use a named Firestore database consistently            | Browser and server lookups must target the same records.                 | Student login, enrolment, reporting and other database-backed workflows.         |
| Keep privileged operations server-side                 | Client code cannot be trusted to grant its own privileges.               | Student authentication, parent provisioning, examinations and protected updates. |
| Remove fake dashboard values                           | Users must not mistake demo data for real platform activity.             | Trustworthiness of dashboards, reports and wallets.                              |
| Enforce permissions in the backend and Firestore rules | Hiding controls in the UI is not sufficient access control.              | School boundaries, student privacy and resource permissions.                     |
| Support legacy records deliberately                    | Historical records may not match the newest schema.                      | Existing student accounts and historical parent/student relationships.           |
| Use server-side timestamps for exams                   | Browser timing and network delays can create inconsistent attempts.      | Exam deadlines, submission state and grading.                                    |
| Validate exam definitions before saving                | Invalid options, duplicate IDs or incorrect marks can corrupt grading.   | Examination correctness and result integrity.                                    |
| Remove brittle build-time source rewriting             | Deployment should compile committed source, not mutate it unpredictably. | Netlify build reliability and maintainability.                                   |
| Align financial interfaces with real workflows         | Demo values and unsupported payout methods are misleading.               | Wallets, withdrawal forms and billing interfaces.                                |
| Design shared portal components consistently           | Many roles share navigation and UI infrastructure.                       | Responsive behaviour, visual consistency and easier maintenance.                 |

## 5. How the development and deployment workflow evolved

The project has not simply involved adding features. A substantial portion of the work has been responding to failures that appeared during compilation, deployment or integration.

The recorded workflow includes:

1. Inspect the repository and identify the failing workflow. Examples include missing Firebase Admin modules, student schedule visibility and examination submission races.
2. Change the relevant implementation. This could mean fixing a type, adding a server endpoint, correcting a Firestore query or changing role validation.
3. Run automated checks. The project has used linting, TypeScript checks, production builds, Firestore Rules Tests and dedicated assessment or student-login compatibility tests.
4. Merge changes into `main` when the applicable review and merge process permits.
5. Verify the deployed application separately. A successful build or merged pull request does not establish that production functions, Firebase rules, indexes and real-user workflows are all working.

That last distinction is especially important. The October records repeatedly note cases where automated checks passed but the Netlify deploy preview was still failing or production acceptance was not yet confirmed.

